      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lenorah was renamed after county clerk Lenorah Epley when postal officials rejected the original name Plainview in 1925.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lenorah_texas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A one‑room school called Plainview opened just south of the Willingham home, with Mittie Walker as its first teacher.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lenorah_texas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s founders were the Willingham family, who arrived around 1906–1908 and are credited with establishing Lenorah.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/lenorah-tx",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Early settlement began in the 1890s with ranchers like Charlie Tom and Hiram Nunn, but the Willinghams are considered the true founders.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lenorah_texas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lenorah lies fourteen miles north of Stanton in central Martin County, placing it in the heart of the Permian Basin.",
